# MongoDB Patterns

## Repository layer

- All queries live in `*.repository.ts`, not in services or controllers
- Return `null` for not-found; let services throw `BusinessException.notFound()`
- For `select: false` fields (passwords, tokens), pass an options object rather
  than dedicated methods, e.g.
  `usersRepository.findById(id, { select: ['password'] })`
- Write/read methods that participate in transactions take an optional trailing
  `session?: ClientSession` and apply it (`.session(session)` on queries,
  `Model.create([data], { session })` for inserts)

## Schema conventions

- `@Schema({ timestamps: true })` on all collections
- Store emails lowercase; normalize at the DTO boundary with `@NormalizeEmail()`
  (schema `lowercase: true` + repo `.toLowerCase()` remain as defense-in-depth)
- Use `sparse: true` for optional unique fields (`googleId`)
- Index frequently queried fields in schema files (e.g. unique
  `(organizationId, email)` on invitations, `(userId, organizationId)` on memberships)

## IDs

- MongoDB `_id` → API `id` (string) via mappers
- Never expose internal fields (`password`, `refreshTokenHash`, reset tokens)

## Transactions

Use `TransactionService.run(session => …)` for multi-document writes — already
applied to org create, org/account delete cascades, invitation accept, and
register-with-invite. Thread the `session` into every repo call inside the unit
of work. Not required for single-document CRUD. **Requires a replica set**
(Atlas `mongodb+srv://` satisfies this; a plain standalone local Mongo will
throw on `withTransaction`).

## Deletes

User-facing deletes are currently **hard deletes** (account/org removal cascade
memberships + invitations within a transaction). `isActive: false` exists on
users for deactivation, but cascades are physical. Soft-delete-everywhere is a
possible future direction, not the current pattern.
